  "live_records": {
    "q-88457": {
      "dom_id": "step-q88457",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{3x}-4x{e}^{3x}-12{e}^{3x}=0?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}-4x{e}^{3x}-12{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x-12){e}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x-12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-4x-12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,6,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 6=-12.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
