    "q-264787": {
      "dom_id": "step-q264787",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}=144\\cdot {5}^{x-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}-144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-144)\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-144=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-4}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-144=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ 4(x+6)(x-6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation $4=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-4}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-4}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
